# VACO -- Docker Compose environment. Copy to `.env` before
# `docker compose up`.
#
# Two required secrets: v4-proxy's Anthropic key, and the
# trusted-service tokens. Every cross-app URL is wired automatically by
# deploy/generate-docker-compose.js from start-ecosystem.sh's manifest,
# so nothing else needs to be set here.

ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# -- Trusted-service allowlist  (REQUIRED) -----------------------------
#
# Five apps receive service-to-service writes and refuse an
# unauthenticated one: V3 (the ledger), VACA (verification grades),
# VACON (the agent registry), VACO Analytics (metric feeds) and
# VACO Notify (the alert channel). They all read the same
# `VACO_SERVICE_TOKENS` allowlist -- see shared/serviceAuth.js.
#
# **This is no longer optional, and the rollout it used to describe is
# finished.** This file previously said "leave both unset and V3 runs in
# 'observe', which changes no behavior" -- and that was true, which was
# the problem. `observe` allows unauthenticated writes. Combined with
# the old `optionalOwnAccount` middleware on the money routes, an
# unauthenticated request drained a stranger's wallet:
#
#   curl -X POST /api/vcoin/transfer \
#     -d '{"fromUserId":"victim","toUserId":"attacker","amount":500}'
#
# That was run against a live instance and it worked. V3 now defaults to
# `enforce`, every calling app sends a service credential, and
# docker-compose marks these `:?` so a missing token stops the stack at
# `up` rather than at the first settlement.
#
# **Two things must agree**, and both are generated from the same
# manifest so they cannot drift:
#
#   VACO_SERVICE_TOKENS   -- the allowlist V3 itself holds
#   VACO_TOKEN_<APP>      -- the token each caller presents
#
# One token per service, never a shared secret: separate tokens mean a
# compromised service is revoked on its own, and rotated without
# coordinating nineteen deploys. Generate each with:
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# The allowlist every receiving app reads. Format: name:token,name:token
VACO_SERVICE_TOKENS=chopz:,chopz-shop:,cvnvo:,dreams:,hvntz:,v4-proxy:,vaca:,vacay:,vaco-analytics:,vaco-notify:,vaco-operator:,vaco-shell:,vacon:,vacon-c:,vago:,vavlt-stvdios:,venvm:,vex:,void:,voidmagic:,voken:,vsafe:,vulture-flix:,vulture-music:,vulture-pods:,vulture-studios:,vxllage:

# The matching per-caller tokens. Each value must equal that service's
# entry in VACO_SERVICE_TOKENS above. Both blocks are DERIVED, not
# maintained -- `--example` rewrites them from the same scan of the
# source that docker-compose.yml is generated from, and `--check` (run
# in CI) fails if they drift. Do not hand-edit either list.
VACO_TOKEN_CHOPZ=
VACO_TOKEN_CHOPZ_SHOP=
VACO_TOKEN_CVNVO=
VACO_TOKEN_DREAMS=
VACO_TOKEN_HVNTZ=
VACO_TOKEN_V4_PROXY=
VACO_TOKEN_VACA=
VACO_TOKEN_VACAY=
VACO_TOKEN_VACO_ANALYTICS=
VACO_TOKEN_VACO_NOTIFY=
VACO_TOKEN_VACO_OPERATOR=
VACO_TOKEN_VACO_SHELL=
VACO_TOKEN_VACON=
VACO_TOKEN_VACON_C=
VACO_TOKEN_VAGO=
VACO_TOKEN_VAVLT_STVDIOS=
VACO_TOKEN_VENVM=
VACO_TOKEN_VEX=
VACO_TOKEN_VOID=
VACO_TOKEN_VOIDMAGIC=
VACO_TOKEN_VOKEN=
VACO_TOKEN_VSAFE=
VACO_TOKEN_VULTURE_FLIX=
VACO_TOKEN_VULTURE_MUSIC=
VACO_TOKEN_VULTURE_PODS=
VACO_TOKEN_VULTURE_STUDIOS=
VACO_TOKEN_VXLLAGE=

#   node scripts/generate-service-tokens.mjs --example   # refresh this file
#   node scripts/generate-service-tokens.mjs --check     # CI: fail on drift
#   node scripts/generate-service-tokens.mjs --write     # fill .env with real secrets
#
# `--write` fills every line above with fresh random values, so the two
# shapes are consistent by construction rather than by careful copying.
#
# -- Media ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# vaco-media's control plane -- sessions, join grants, the catalogue,
# playback grants -- is real and runs with no configuration at all. What
# these switch on is the ability to actually carry bytes.
#
# Both default to an adapter that does the authorization work and then
# says plainly that nothing will connect or play. That is deliberate: a
# stack that comes up pretending is worse than one that comes up honest.
#
#   VACO_MEDIA_TRANSPORT=livekit   + LIVEKIT_* -> real audio/video
#   VACO_MEDIA_STORAGE=s3          + S3_*      -> real playback
#
# LiveKit is self-hostable; the same token works against your own
# instance or a managed one. VACO_MEDIA_S3_* is any S3-compatible store
# -- MinIO or SeaweedFS on your own hardware, or a hosted bucket.
#
# **VACO_MEDIA_CDN_BASE is not optional at scale.** Serving video from
# one origin to a real audience is ruinous. Without it the signed URL
# points straight at the bucket, which is fine for audio and testing and
# nothing else.
# VACO_MEDIA_TRANSPORT=loopback
# LIVEKIT_URL=
# LIVEKIT_API_KEY=
# LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=
# VACO_MEDIA_STORAGE=local
# VACO_MEDIA_S3_ENDPOINT=
# VACO_MEDIA_S3_BUCKET=
# VACO_MEDIA_S3_ACCESS_KEY=
# VACO_MEDIA_S3_SECRET_KEY=
# VACO_MEDIA_CDN_BASE=

# -- Operator authority --------------------------------------------------
#
# **The one secret that does not stop the stack if you forget it, and
# should.** VACO_OPERATOR_BOOTSTRAP seeds the first operator in
# vaco-operator. It is generated with `:-` rather than `:?` in the
# compose file on purpose: "no operators yet" is the correct starting
# state for an authority service, and one that invents its own first
# authority is not one.
#
# The consequence, stated plainly: leave this empty and the stack comes
# up perfectly clean with **every group-2 route dead** -- 27 settlement,
# grading, vetting and refund routes returning 403, because no operator
# exists to hold any scope. Nothing warns you. Check it after deploy:
#
#   curl localhost:8820/api/coverage     # operators: 0 means nobody can decide
#
# Generate one the same way as a service token:
#   node -e "console.log('vop_'+require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
# The bootstrap holds `operator:grant` and nothing else -- it cannot
# settle, grade or vet. Its only job is to create real operators and
# grant them real scopes. See dev-docs/OPERATOR_ROLES_SCOPE.md §3.5.
VACO_OPERATOR_BOOTSTRAP=

# vaco-audit and vaco-operator both default to `enforce`, which makes
# them hard dependencies of every group-2 route: if either is down,
# those routes refuse rather than proceed unrecorded or unverified.
# `observe` is the lever for riding out an incident -- and for the
# operator check it is far more dangerous than for the audit log, since
# it admits unverified callers. Neither is a resting state.
# VACO_AUDIT_MODE=enforce
# VACO_OPERATOR_MODE=enforce

# Modes, for reference. `enforce` is the default and the right value in
# production. `observe` (allow, but record unauthenticated callers on
# GET /api/health) is the tool for onboarding a NEW caller without
# breaking it first -- not a resting state. `off` disables the check.
# VACO_SERVICE_AUTH_MODE=enforce

# -- VACON-C's Postgres -------------------------------------------------
#
# VACON-C is the one app that does not persist through
# createPersistentStore. It is a tick simulation whose durable record is
# Postgres: server/persistence.js loads the world before the server
# listens, and checkpoints it every VACONC_CHECKPOINT_TICKS ticks.
#
# **It fails soft without this.** Given no reachable database VACON-C
# starts an empty world, says so in full on stderr, and then never
# checkpoints -- so the simulation runs and nothing is saved. That is a
# deliberate choice (an app that refuses to boot because its archive is
# down is worse), but it means an unset DATABASE_URL is a silent data
# loss rather than a startup failure. Set it.
#
# docker-compose.yml supplies this automatically from POSTGRES_PASSWORD
# and its own `postgres` service; this variable is for running VACON-C
# outside Compose.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://vacancy:vacancy_dev@localhost:5432/vacancy

# The password for that service. No default anywhere -- docker-compose
# refuses to start without it rather than shipping a working one.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# Ticks between checkpoints. A checkpoint rewrites every table, so this
# trades write cost against how much history a crash can lose.
# VACONC_CHECKPOINT_TICKS=10
